WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
The first film showed the gut wall keeping its own beat.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.840
The stomach runs one more machine: a pump.

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.080
Its juice reaches about pH 1.

4
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.980
That is roughly a million times more acidic than inside the cell making it.

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.580
What switches that pump on, and what switches it off?

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:27.753
At rest, the parietal cell keeps its pumps tucked inside small vesicles.

7
00:00:29.013 --> 00:00:33.873
When stimulated, it moves them into the membrane of a deep fold facing the lumen.

8
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:40.133
Each cycle swaps a hydrogen ion out for a potassium ion, paid for with ATP.

9
00:00:41.573 --> 00:00:45.393
Chloride follows potassium out, so hydrochloric acid builds.

10
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:51.747
Three signals turn the pump on: acetylcholine from nerves, histamine, and the

11
00:00:51.747 --> 00:00:55.987
hormone gastrin. The vagus nerve can start acid early.

12
00:00:57.607 --> 00:01:00.967
Sight, smell, taste and chewing switch it on before food arrives.

13
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:06.847
Then food stretches the stomach, releasing more acetylcholine and gastrin.

14
00:01:08.207 --> 00:01:12.427
Digested protein also acts directly on G cells in the antrum.

15
00:01:13.747 --> 00:01:17.587
Gastrin travels in the blood to the body of the stomach, and acid climbs.

16
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:20.267
Here is the one idea.

17
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:24.427
Food arriving in a part of the gut turns that part on.

18
00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:29.287
What that part makes, or passes downstream, turns it back down.

19
00:01:29.287 --> 00:01:31.967
The stomach shows both halves.

20
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:36.857
Acid made in the body of the stomach flows down to the antrum.

21
00:01:38.057 --> 00:01:40.937
There, acid makes D cells release somatostatin.

22
00:01:42.497 --> 00:01:45.477
Somatostatin holds back the G cells beside them.

23
00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:48.357
The more acid, the less gastrin.

24
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:54.747
Now the acidic, fatty chyme crosses into the duodenum, the first part of the small

25
00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:59.587
intestine. Will the duodenum call for more stomach acid, or less?

26
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:11.103
Less. Acid in the duodenum releases secretin from S cells.

27
00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:16.203
Fat and digested protein release CCK from I cells.

28
00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:19.143
Both inhibit gastric acid.

29
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:23.083
CCK probably works through somatostatin.

30
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:26.553
So the meal switches acid on in the stomach.

31
00:02:27.793 --> 00:02:32.873
It switches acid off from two places downstream of the pump: the antrum and the

32
00:02:32.873 --> 00:02:38.213
duodenum. Once chyme reaches the intestine, the signals are mostly a brake.

33
00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:41.297
The lining must survive the acid.

34
00:02:42.877 --> 00:02:47.217
Surface cells secrete bicarbonate, and a mucus gel traps it against the lining.

35
00:02:48.417 --> 00:02:51.097
So the surface stays far less acidic than the lumen.

36
00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:55.437
Tight junctions limit how much acid leaks back in.

37
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.320
So acid is turned on by what arrives, and down by what the stomach sends onward.

38
00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.260
Acid brakes it from the antrum.

39
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.600
Acid and fat brake it from the duodenum.
